Consider the following classes of finitely presented groups, arising from fundamental groups of complements of codimension-2 embeddings:
- K_0 = the class consisting of the infinite cyclic group Z (groups of complements of S^0 in S^2);
- K_1 = the class of groups of complements of smooth embedded 1-spheres in S^3 (classical knot groups);
- K_2 = the class of groups of complements of smooth embedded 2-spheres in S^4 (2-knot groups);
- K_3 = the class of groups of complements of smooth embedded 3-spheres in S^5 (equivalently, finitely presented groups G with H_1(G) ≅ Z, H_2(G) = 0, and admitting an element whose normal closure is G);
- S = the class of groups of complements of orientable closed surfaces smoothly embedded in S^4;
- M = the class of groups of complements of smooth embeddings into simply connected closed 4-manifolds;
- G = the class of all finitely presented groups.

These satisfy K_0 ⊊ K_1 ⊊ K_2 ⊊ K_3 ⊊ S ⊊ M ⊊ G.

It is known that whenever A, B ∈ {K_0, K_1, K_2, K_3, S, M, G} with B ⊊ A and K_3 ⊆ A, there is no algorithm that, given a finite presentation of a group G known to lie in A, decides whether G ∈ B.

Question: Does the same undecidability statement hold when A = K_2? That is, for every B ∈ {K_0, K_1} (the proper subclasses of K_2 in the list), is it true that there is no algorithm that, given a finite presentation of a 2-knot group, decides membership in B?

Equivalently (as a sufficient condition): does there exist a 2-knot group (a member of K_2) with unsolvable word problem?

